"p3_sumcheck::layout - Rust (https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/p3_sumcheck/layout/index.html)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/p3_sumcheck/layout/index.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: 【0†iframe】【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: ## 【2†Module layout】\nL3: \nL4: ## 【3†p3_sumcheck】0.8.0\nL5: \nL6: ## 【2†Module layout】\nL7: \nL8: ### 【2†Sections】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【4†Why stack】\nL11:   * 【5†Layout of the stacked polynomial】\nL12:   * 【6†Selectors: addressing a slot by a boolean prefix】\nL13:   * 【7†Why selector lifts stay cheap in WHIR】\nL14:   * 【8†Residual sumcheck: two binding modes】\nL15:   * 【9†References】\nL16: \nL17: ### 【10†Module Items】\nL18: \nL19:   * 【10†Re-exports】\nL20:   * 【11†Structs】\nL21:   * 【12†Enums】\nL22:   * 【13†Traits】\nL23:   * 【14†Functions】\nL24:   * 【15†Type Aliases】\nL25: \nL26: ## 【3†In crate p3_sumcheck】\nL27: # Module layout [Button: Copy item path]\nL28: \nL29: 【16†Source】\nL30: \nL31: Expand description\nL32: \nL33: Stacked sumcheck layout.\nL34: ## 【4†§】Why stack\nL35: \nL36:   * A WHIR commitment carries a fixed per-commitment overhead (FFT + Merkle).\nL37:   * Committing each table separately multiplies that overhead by the table count.\nL38:   * Stacking concatenates every table into one multilinear polynomial and commits once, so a single commitment covers all tables.\nL39:   * WHIR natively supports multiple opening claims on the committed polynomial, so no extra batching sumcheck is needed on top.\nL40: ## 【5†§】Layout of the stacked polynomial\nL41: \nL42:   * Sort source tables by arity, largest first.\nL43:   * Lay columns out back-to-back on the boolean hypercube.\nL44:   * Each column takes a contiguous slot of size `2^arity`.\nL45:   * Pad with zeros up to the next power of two.\nL46:   * The concatenation is itself a multilinear polynomial.\nL47: Example: three tables with `(4, 3, 2)` variables and one column each.\nL48: \nL49:     `    +---- 16 ----+-- 8 --+-- 4 --+-- pad --+\nL50:         |    P_1     |  P_2  |  P_3  |  zeros  |\nL51:         +------------+-------+-------+---------+\nL52:         0           16      24      28        32`\nL53: ## 【6†§】Selectors: addressing a slot by a boolean prefix\nL54: \nL55:   * Each column is reached by prefixing its local variables with a boolean selector that picks the slot.\nL56:   * Local variables follow the selector bits.\nL57: \nL58: For the example above, with `P` the stacked polynomial:\nL59: \nL60:     `    P_1(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4) = P(0,       x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4)\nL61:         P_2(x_1, x_2, x_3)      = P(1, 0,    x_1, x_2, x_3)\nL62:         P_3(x_1, x_2)           = P(1, 1, 0, x_1, x_2)`\nL63: ## 【7†§】Why selector lifts stay cheap in WHIR\nL64: \nL65:   * The WHIR cost of adding an equality constraint `P(z) = y` scales as `O(2^k)`, where `k` counts the coordinates of `z` that are not in `{0, 1}`.\nL66:   * Selector coordinates are always boolean, so they do not inflate `k`.\nL67:   * Lifting a local claim into a stacked claim therefore adds no asymptotic cost beyond the original local coordinates.\nL68: ## 【8†§】Residual sumcheck: two binding modes\nL69: \nL70: After lifting, a batching challenge `alpha` collapses every recorded opening into one residual claim. The residual sumcheck can bind variables in two different orders. In both modes, SVO accumulators are precomputed at claim-recording time and every folding round is driven from them with Lagrange weights; the modes differ only in the handoff to the residual product polynomial once folding completes:\nL71:   * Prefix-first binding chooses packed or scalar compression from the residual width.\nL72:   * Suffix-first binding: the handoff is unpacked (`compress_stacked`).\nL73: \nL74: Both modes end at the same residual product polynomial; the binding order only decides which fast-path tricks apply on the handoff.\nL75: ## 【9†§】References\nL76: \nL77:   * Stacking construction: “Minimal zkVM for Lean Ethereum”, section 3.7.\nL78:   * WHIR proximity argument: ePrint 2024/1586.\nL79:   * SVO accumulators: ePrint 2025/1117, Algorithm 5.\nL80:   * Jagged PCS: ePrint 2025/0917.\nL81: \nL82: ## Re-exports【10†§】\nL83: \nL84: `pub use crate::table::【17†TableShape】;`\nL85: ## Structs【11†§】\nL86: 【18†ColumnOut】\nL87:     One logical column’s disjoint destination inside a suffix-layout stack.\nL88: 【19†EqPartials】\nL89:     Equality-weight partial-evaluation table produced for one round of the small-value-optimization preprocessing.\nL90: 【20†EqSvoPartials】\nL91:     Equality-weight preprocessing payload for one ordinary opening.\nL92: 【21†FilledSuffixWitness】\nL93:     A directly filled suffix-layout polynomial and its verifier-reconstructible metadata.\nL94: 【22†LayoutStrategy】\nL95:     Verifier-side metadata required to replay a committed layout.\nL96: 【23†MultiClaim】\nL97:     A batch of openings that share one evaluation point.\nL98: 【24†NextPartials】\nL99:     Preprocessing residuals for one round of a repeat-last successor opening.\nL100: 【25†NextSvoPartials】\nL101:     Preprocessing payload for one repeat-last successor opening.\nL102: 【26†Opening】\nL103:     Single opening of one polynomial at a shared evaluation point.\nL104: 【27†PrefixProver】\nL105:     Stacked-sumcheck prover with prefix-first variable binding.\nL106: 【28†Selector】\nL107:     Identifies one slot inside the stacked polynomial.\nL108: 【29†StackedClaims】\nL109:     Opening claims recorded against one stacked polynomial, shared by both binding modes.\nL110: 【30†SuffixLayoutPlan】\nL111:     A reusable immutable placement plan for directly filled suffix-layout witnesses.\nL112: 【31†SuffixProver】\nL113:     Stacked-sumcheck prover with suffix-first variable binding.\nL114: 【32†SuffixResidualProver】\nL115:     Residual sumcheck prover over the stacked polynomial, with its tables in `R`.\nL116: 【33†Table】\nL117:     A column-major table of multilinear polynomials sharing a common arity.\nL118: 【34†TablePlacement】\nL119:     Placement metadata for one table inside the stacked polynomial.\nL120: 【35†Verifier】\nL121:     Verifier-side layout and claim registry.\nL122: 【36†Witness】\nL123:     Owns the source tables together with their placement in the stacked polynomial.\nL124: ## Enums【12†§】\nL125: \nL126: 【37†ColumnView】\nL127:     A borrowed, representation-independent view of one logical table column.\nL128: 【38†SuffixFillError】\nL129:     A source batch that disagrees with its immutable placement plan.\nL130: 【39†SuffixLayoutPlanError】\nL131:     Dimensions that cannot form a suffix-layout witness.\nL132: \nL133: ## Traits【13†§】\nL134: \nL135: 【40†Layout】\nL136:     Stacked-sumcheck prover layout\nL137: 【41†SuffixTableSource】\nL138:     A source that writes one table directly into its final contiguous suffix-layout slots.\nL139: ## Functions【14†§】\nL140: \nL141: 【42†commitment_domain_separator】\nL142:     Describe the phase that binds the stacked commitment.\nL143: 【43†observe_commitment】\nL144:     Bind the Merkle root the stacked codeword is committed under.\nL145: 【44†plan_stacked_layout】\nL146:     Plan the stacked layout of a set of committed table shapes.\nL147: ## Type Aliases【15†§】\nL148: \nL149: 【45†ProverMultiClaim】\nL150:     Multi-opening claim over an SVO point.\nL151: 【46†ProverVirtualClaim】\nL152:     Virtual claim carrying precomputed SVO accumulators.\nL153: 【47†VerifierMultiClaim】\nL154:     Multi-opening claim over a plain point on the verifier side.\nL155: 【48†VerifierOpening】\nL156:     Opening on the verifier side: column index plus claimed evaluation.\nL157: 【49†VerifierVirtualClaim】\nL158:     Virtual evaluation claim on the stacked polynomial (verifier side).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInteractive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITC.2026.3)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Cr