 * 【59†SAT】\nL63:     * 【60†SEA】\nL64:     * 【61†SLATE】\nL65:     * 【62†SNAPL】\nL66:     * 【63†SoCG】\nL67:     * 【64†STACS】\nL68:     * 【65†SWAT】\nL69:     * 【66†TIME】\nL70:     * 【67†Tokenomics】\nL71:     * 【68†TQC】\nL72:     * 【69†TYPES】\nL73:     * 【70†WABI】\nL74:     * 【71†WCET】\nL75: \nL76:   * 【0†Artifacts】\nL77:     * 【72†Supplementary Materials (Software, Datasets, ...)】\nL78:     * 【73†dblp Artifacts】\nL79:     *     * 【11†DARTS (Evaluated Artifacts)】\nL80: \nL81: Document 【74†Image: Open Access Logo】\nL82: \nL83: [Input]\nL84: \nL85: `https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITC.2026.3`\nL86:   * 【75†Export XML】\nL87:   * 【76†Export ACM-XML】\nL88:   * 【77†Export DOAJ-XML】\nL89:   * 【78†Export Schema.org】\nL90:   * 【79†Export BibTeX】\nL91: \nL92: * * *\nL93: # Interactive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation\nL94: ### Authors 【80†Gal Arnon】 , 【81†Alessandro Chiesa】 , 【82†Giacomo Fenzi】 , 【83†Eylon Yogev】\nL95: \nL96: * * *\nL97: \nL98:   * Part of: Volume: 【84†7th Conference on Information-Theoretic Cryptography (ITC 2026)】\nL99: Part of: Series: 【6†Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs)】\nL100: Part of: Conference: 【48†Conference on Information-Theoretic Cryptography (ITC)】\nL101:   * License:   【85†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license†creativecommons.org】\nL102:   * Publication Date: 2026-08-12\nL103: \nL104: * * *\nL105: \nL106: 【86†PDF】\nL107: ## File\nL108: \nL109: 【86†PDF LIPIcs.ITC.2026.3.pdf】\nL110: \nL111:   * Filesize: 0.81 MB\nL112:   * 19 pages\nL113: \nL114: ## Document Identifiers\nL115: \nL116:   * DOI: 【87†10.4230/LIPIcs.ITC.2026.3†doi.org】\nL117:   * URN: 【88†urn:nbn:de:0030-drops-270964†nbn-resolving.org】\nL118: \nL119: ## Subject Classification\nL120: \nL121: ##### ACM Subject Classification\nL122: \nL123:   * Theory of computation → Interactive proof systems\nL124:   * Theory of computation → Cryptographic protocols\nL125: \nL126: ##### Keywords\nL127: \nL128:   * interactive proofs\nL129:   * polynomial evaluation\nL130: ## Metrics\nL131: \nL132:   * 【0†Access Statistics】\nL133:   * Total Accesses (updated on a weekly basis)\nL134: \nL135: 0\nL136: \nL137: Document\nL138: \nL139: 0\nL140: \nL141: Metadata\nL142: \nL143: ##\nL144: ## Abstract\nL145: \nL146:     Polynomials are a fundamental mathematical object underlying virtually all of theoretical computer science. In proof systems, a common task for the verifier is to evaluate a polynomial of degree d at m distinct points. The best known algorithm for this problem performs O((m + d) ⋅ log²(m + d)) field operations.\nL147:     We present a concretely efficient MA protocol for this problem in which the verifier runs in linear time: the prover sends a single message consisting of d - 1 field elements, and the verifier performs only O(m + d) field operations. We further extend our protocol to handle the more general setting of evaluating multiple polynomials at multiple points, and for this problem, we construct an AMA protocol.\nL148:     Our protocols improve the verifier time in several interactive proofs. Most notable are the sumcheck protocol over a large summation domain and protocols that rely on polynomial quotienting. In particular, by a straightforward application of our results, we reduce the verifier’s runtime in the STIR protocol (CRYPTO 2024) to match that of WHIR (EUROCRYPT 2025), despite WHIR being highly optimized for verification time.\nL149:     As an additional application, we show that any univariate polynomial commitment scheme (PCS) can be transformed, in a black-box manner, into a new scheme that efficiently supports batch openings at multiple points. In particular, opening m points incurs only a constant overhead compared to opening a single point.\nL150: ## Cite As Get BibTex\nL151: \nL152: Gal Arnon, Alessandro Chiesa, Giacomo Fenzi, and Eylon Yogev. Interactive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation. In 7th Conference on Information-Theoretic Cryptography (ITC 2026). Leibniz International Proc